{
  "validity": "pass",
  "quality": "adequate",
  "issues": [
    {
      "kind": "quality",
      "location": "roles: membership_robert_walsh / membership_robert_chen",
      "problem": "Both Robert candidates (and essentially every seeded user) hold membership in the single workspace T01WORKSPACE, so the WORKSPACE_MEMBERSHIP hop performs no actual disambiguating work — it is present only to satisfy the route requirement, not because it constrains which Robert or which channel is relevant.",
      "repair": "Either note explicitly in the design that this hop is structural rather than discriminating, or (if genuine discrimination is desired) seed a workspace-membership distinction between the two Roberts to make the route meaningfully load-bearing."
    },
    {
      "kind": "quality",
      "location": "roles: conv_general vs conv_engineering",
      "problem": "The two alternative matches are not equally strong: Robert Chen's message ('When will the PyTorch rewrite be ready?') is an unambiguous 'something we're building' + 'ready' match, while Robert Walsh's message ('when the MCP deployment will be done') conflates deployment with building, making the two alternatives slightly asymmetric in plausibility.",
      "repair": "Strengthen the general-channel message wording (e.g., 'when will the onboarding feature we're building be done') to make both alternatives equally strong instances of 'something we're building', tightening the intended tie."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The case correctly builds a clean underspecified ambiguity: two workspace members display-named 'Robert' (Robert Walsh, Robert Chen) each authored a message matching the 'ready or done' + 'something we're building' pattern, in two distinct channels (general, engineering), with no third Robert or stray 'ready/done' message from either user found in the seed. The Nick 'ready' negative and the reaction-based negative correctly guard against wrong-identity and wrong-relationship-role false matches. Access via U01AGENBOT9 (member of workspace and both candidate channels) using documented conversations.list/users.conversations/conversations.history is realistic and sufficient to discover the ambiguity without any hidden ID assumption. The route CONVERSATION→WORKSPACE→WORKSPACE_MEMBERSHIP→USER is nominally exercised, though it does no real narrowing since every user is a member of the sole seeded workspace — this is a quality softness, not a validity defect, since the traversal is still genuinely present and consistent with the exposure_qualification's witness definition. Minor asymmetry in how strongly each candidate message evokes 'something we're building' is a wording quality nit rather than a correctness flaw."
}

{
  "validity": "pass",
  "quality": "adequate",
  "issues": [
    {
      "kind": "quality",
      "location": "selector.focal (channels->teams->user_teams->users, filter display_name='Robert')",
      "problem": "The focal route hop (workspace membership) is non-discriminating by construction: it matches every channel in T01WORKSPACE regardless of which Robert is meant, since all users including both Roberts are members of the single seeded workspace. All actual disambiguation is done by the auxiliary messages-based clause, not by the assigned route's own filter.",
      "repair": "Accept as a structural/route-compliance hop (already documented in notes) or, if genuine discrimination is wanted, seed a second workspace so membership actually varies between the two Roberts."
    },
    {
      "kind": "quality",
      "location": "auxiliary filter messages.message_text contains_ci 'when'",
      "problem": "The keyword 'when' is used as a coarse proxy for 'asked when X would be ready or done'; verified against the seed it produces exactly the two intended messages with no false positives, but the proxy is fragile in general (would break if any other Robert message anywhere contained 'when').",
      "repair": "No change needed for this seed since verification confirms exact match; note for future seeds that keyword proxies should be checked against all messages by scoped authors, not just the intended ones."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Compilation faithfully implements the design: bindings mirror the declared roles, the mechanical query (channels→teams→user_teams→users filtered on display_name='Robert', intersected with channels→messages→users filtered on display_name='Robert' and message_text containing 'when') produces exactly the two intended channels (C01ABCD1234, C03IJKL9012) with no unintended extra matches — confirmed by manually checking every message authored by U03ROBERT23 and U_ROBERT for the 'when' substring, which yields exactly one qualifying message per user. The Nick Fury 'ready' message (C04MNOP3456) is correctly excluded by the author-identity filter alone (not even reaching the keyword check), matching the declared near-miss. mechanical_checks report no errors and computed_matches equal the two designed candidates, so no accidental extra matches or dropped alternatives exist. The remaining concerns (non-discriminating membership hop, generality of the keyword proxy) are inherited quality softness from the design stage, not compilation defects, and do not change the correctness of the constructed case."
}

